# Writing Standard

This standard applies to every piece of prose the team produces: research reports, technical documents, README files, PR descriptions, iteration logs, and user-facing copy. Code comments and docstrings follow Code Principles instead.

## Core Rule

Every word must earn its place. Use plain, precise language to deliver core points through clear, self-consistent logic. Avoid obscure vocabulary, exaggerated phrasing, and complex compound sentences that increase cognitive load. Technical terms from the relevant domain are expected and welcome; jargon for its own sake is not. Keep sentences short enough to parse in one pass. Between any two consecutive sentences, the logical connection must be obvious. Between any two consecutive paragraphs, the narrative must flow without a gap. The result should read smoothly for both domain experts and general readers, reaching the clarity and rigour of a top-tier academic venue while remaining accessible.

## Prohibited Patterns

Bold text for emphasis. Structure and word choice should do that work. If a sentence needs bold to convey importance, the sentence is too weak.

Em-dashes used as loose connectors. They signal that the writer has not decided on the precise relationship between clauses. Replace with a period, a comma, or a subordinate conjunction that makes the relationship explicit.

Numbered or bulleted lists where a paragraph would convey the idea more naturally. Lists are appropriate for genuinely parallel items (steps, options, requirements). They are not a substitute for developing an argument or explaining a causal chain.

Vague assertions that leave the reader unsure what was actually said. Every claim should be concrete enough that the reader can determine whether they agree or disagree. "The system is highly scalable" says nothing; "the system handles 10x traffic increase with linear cost growth" says something.

If a draft exhibits any of these, revise before delivering.

## Self-Check

Before finalising any document, read the text as if encountering it for the first time. Verify that each sentence follows naturally from the previous one and that each paragraph advances the narrative. If any transition feels abrupt, insert a bridging sentence or restructure the surrounding paragraphs. The goal is a reading experience where the reader never has to backtrack to understand context.

